// Fixture: op_statement_missing_leading_field_error
// Rule under test: newly resolved rule (now written into the spec) -- every statement
// must begin with a field name, no exceptions. The three legal statement shapes are:
//   1. `field = expr`   (assign)
//   2. `field op expr`  (op-statement)
//   3. `field`          (bare, modify-only scope entry)
// A line that does not start with a field name -- no leading identifier, no `=` -- is a
// SYNTAX error at phase 3 (Parse), not a later-phase error, since the parser cannot
// determine which field the statement even targets. This is a different error category
// from most of this corpus (which mostly targets phase 5/6/8 semantic errors) -- this
// one is caught before any type registration or resolution would even begin.
//
// This is also *why* self-reference in non-leading position can only be written via
// assign, never op-statement shorthand: op-statement syntax requires the field name to
// lead the statement by construction. See crossfield_self_reference_non_leading.gddl
// (crossfield references group) for the corresponding valid case, written correctly
// via assign.

define Creature
	hitpoints_maximum = f32

Creature Base
	hitpoints_maximum = 100

Creature Broken = Base
	20 + hitpoints_maximum * 0.5
	// ERROR: this line has no leading field name and no `=`. It is not valid
	// op-statement shorthand (op-statement requires the field to lead: `field op
	// expr...`), and it is not a valid bare-field scope entry either (bare field
	// syntax is just a field name, nothing else on the line). There is no legal
	// statement shape this line matches -- the parser has no field to attach this
	// expression to.

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Compile error against Broken, reported at phase 3 (Parse): statement does not
//   begin with a field name -- no valid statement shape matched. Should surface
//   before phase 5 (Validate) or phase 6 (Resolve) ever run, since the parser cannot
//   even build a valid AST node for this line.
